DeepSeek Harness (dsh) — Debian/Ubuntu package notes
====================================================

What this package installs
--------------------------
  /usr/bin/dsh                       launcher (requires system Node.js >= 24.9)
  /usr/bin/dsh-web-url               prints the current tokenized Web UI URL
  /usr/lib/dsh/app/                  pinned DSH production installation
  /usr/lib/dsh/bin/pnpm              bundled pnpm used by plugin management
  /usr/lib/dsh/pnpm/                 the pinned pnpm installation itself
  /usr/lib/systemd/user/dsh-web.service
                                     systemd *user* unit for the Web UI
  /usr/share/doc/deepseek-harness/   this note, copyright, third-party notices
  /usr/share/man/man1/dsh.1.gz

Runtime requirement
-------------------
This package does not bundle Node.js. It depends on the system package
`nodejs (>= 24.9.0)`. Ubuntu 24.04 ships Node.js 18, which is too old, so
install Node 24 from NodeSource before installing this package:

  sudo apt-get install -y curl ca-certificates
  curl -fsSL https://deb.nodesource.com/setup_24.x | sudo -E bash -
  sudo apt-get install -y nodejs
  node --version        # must be >= 24.9.0

If `apt` refuses the package with an unmet `nodejs (>= 24.9.0)` dependency,
that check is doing its job: fix Node first, then reinstall.

The user service (never root)
-----------------------------
dsh-web is a systemd *user* unit. It runs as the user who enables it, with
that user's $HOME and ~/.dsh. There is no system unit in /lib/systemd/system,
so nothing starts as root and no root-run server is listening on port 3080.

If the package is installed with sudo (or pkexec), the maintainer script
enables and starts the unit for the installing user automatically. If it
cannot reach that user's systemd manager (common in containers and image
builds), the unit is still installed; start it after logging in:

  systemctl --user enable --now dsh-web

Useful commands:

  systemctl --user status dsh-web
  systemctl --user restart dsh-web
  systemctl --user stop dsh-web
  journalctl --user -u dsh-web -f
  dsh-web-url

The Web UI requires a per-process launch token on the first visit. The service
prints the tokenized URL to the user journal at every start; dsh-web-url prints
the most recent one. Opening that URL mints a persistent browser cookie, after
which http://127.0.0.1:3080 works directly.

The service starts when the user logs in and stops when the user's systemd
manager stops (normally at logout). To keep it running without an active
login, enable lingering for that user explicitly:

  sudo loginctl enable-linger <user>

Configuration
-------------
The unit listens on 127.0.0.1:3080 by default and passes --no-open. Put
environment variables such as DEEPSEEK_API_KEY or DSH_HOME in the optional
file:

  ~/.config/dsh/web.env

To change the bind address or port, use a drop-in instead of editing the
packaged unit:

  systemctl --user edit dsh-web
  # [Service]
  # ExecStart=
  # ExecStart=/usr/bin/dsh web --no-open --host 0.0.0.0 --port 3080

The unit deliberately binds loopback only. For remote access prefer an SSH
tunnel over binding a public interface:

  ssh -L 3080:127.0.0.1:3080 user@host

CLI usage
---------
  dsh web                 start the Web UI in the foreground
  dsh web --help          Web UI flags (--host, --port, --no-open, ...)
  dsh --version           version
  dsh plugin --help       plugin management (uses the bundled pnpm)

Plugins (per user)
------------------
Plugins are not installed by apt. They belong to each user and live in that
user's profile directory:

  ~/.dsh/profiles/web/package.json      installed bundle dependencies
  ~/.dsh/profiles/web/pnpm-lock.yaml    exact versions
  ~/.dsh/profiles/web/node_modules/     plugin packages
  ~/.dsh/profiles/web/cordis.patch.yml  the user's own overrides

The package bundles a pinned pnpm under /usr/lib/dsh and puts
/usr/lib/dsh/bin first on PATH (through both the launcher and
dsh-web.service), so no separate pnpm install is needed:

  dsh plugin --profile web add <package>
  dsh plugin --profile web list
  dsh plugin --profile web remove <package>
  systemctl --user restart dsh-web

The same operations are available under Plugins in the Web UI. That page
manages the running profile and reports whether a change applied live or
needs the restart above.

Do not run these commands with sudo: the profile belongs to the user, and
root-owned files under ~/.dsh break the user service. Plugins execute inside
that user's Host process with that user's privileges and can read its stored
credentials, so install only bundles you trust.

Upgrading or removing deepseek-harness never touches ~/.dsh, so plugins,
profiles, and lockfiles survive; `apt purge` keeps them as well.

User data
---------
Sessions, profiles, credentials, and settings live in ~/.dsh (or $DSH_HOME).
They belong to the user, not to the package, and are never removed by
`apt remove --purge`. Remove ~/.dsh yourself if you want a clean reset.

Package name and conflict
-------------------------
The package is named deepseek-harness, not dsh: Debian/Ubuntu already have an
unrelated package called `dsh` (distributed shell) that owns /usr/bin/dsh.
This package Conflicts/Replaces it. If that package is installed, apt asks to
remove it before installing this one.

Removal
-------
  sudo apt remove deepseek-harness
  # or, to also drop package-owned configuration (user data is still kept):
  sudo apt purge deepseek-harness

Removal stops and disables dsh-web for the installing user. Other users who
enabled the unit themselves should run `systemctl --user disable --now dsh-web`
before the package is removed.

Source
------
Upstream: https://github.com/deepseek-ai/deepseek-harness
The exact tag and commit used for this build are recorded in
/usr/share/doc/deepseek-harness/BUILD-INFO.json and in the package's
X-DSH-Source-Commit control field.
